// The contract between the hangar core and a plugin.
//
// Everything a person can ask for is a plugin. A plugin DECLARES what it
// offers (Describe) and ACTS when the core asks it to (Configure, Plan,
// Create, Delete, Act, Reconcile). It never calls the core, it never sees
// another plugin's credential, and it never decides who may ask: identity,
// tiers, limits, the registry and the audit belong to the core.
//
// Values that are JSON documents travel as bytes holding JSON (UTF-8): specs,
// params, observed state, schemas. A JSON number stays a number that way,
// which a google.protobuf.Struct would turn into a double.
//
// Four rules a plugin must keep (ARCHITECTURE.md, "The plugin contract"):
//
//  1. Create, Delete and Act are IDEMPOTENT on the resource id. The core may
//     call them again after a crash, with the same resource and the same
//     params, and must get the same outcome — the plugin finds the engine
//     object it already made (by the id it wrote on it) instead of making a
//     second one. Delete of something already gone succeeds.
//  2. Plan has no side effects. The core calls it to learn what a request
//     would hold before it admits the request.
//  3. An action's params are absolute ("memory 8"), never relative
//     ("memory +2"), so that rule 1 holds for actions too.
//  4. Refusals are gRPC statuses: INVALID_ARGUMENT for a request that can
//     never succeed as written, FAILED_PRECONDITION for one the engine refuses
//     in its present state, UNAVAILABLE when the engine cannot be reached (the
//     core retries). Anything else is a failure.

